// a guard binds a name that is only meaningful on the path where the value was there. an else arm that
// ran on and rejoined would leave that name bound to nothing at all, so the arm has to leave - and
// refusing at the declaration is what makes the binding's promise true by construction

function lookup(int32 $k) : int32? { return null; }

guard int32 $v = lookup(1) else { echo 0; }

echo $v;
